  "query_preview": "..\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nCurrently, high pressure is causing the risk of fog, due to\nsubsidence. Visibilities may quickly drop to less than a quarter \nmile in some areas, which may cause impacts to the Tuesday morning\ncommute. A Dense Fog Advisory may be issued should the current \nforecast hold through the early morning hours of Tuesday. \n\nAn upper-level ridge building to our west will usher in dry and \nabove average temperatures as a upper-level trough translates \neastward. Dry and warm weather conditions are expected throughout \nmost of the holiday week. Come Friday, expect gusty conditions as \na front moves through the region. By Friday night/Saturday \nmorning, expect a passage of another front, associated with a \nsurface low. This frontal passage will persist breezy conditions \ninto Saturday evening. This brings the potential for\nprecipitation; current model guidance contains too much \ndisagreement to accurately provide any potential QPF at this \nmoment.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & NBM DEVIATIONS:\n\nThere were no major deviations to the forecast for this evening's\nforecast. With respect to forecast confidence, there is a high \n(75%-80%) confidence in the expected ridging and warming trend, \nand lower (15%-25%) confidence in troughing and potential \nprecipitation through the coming weekend.\n\nFINDLEY",
